{"candidates":[{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"A decentralized network of small services is more resilient to correlated failure than a redundant monolith if and only if the entropy of its dependency graph exceeds the monolith's internal diversity coefficient.","counterconditions":["The decentralized services adopt a standardized 'industry best practice' stack (e.g., all on the same K8s version/cloud region), effectively creating a virtual monolith.","The monolith employs N-version programming or diverse hardware substrates that exceed the operational diversity of the decentralized network."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The 'Shadow Monolith' mechanism: decentralization's resilience is not derived from the number of operators, but from the lack of shared systemic triggers (e.g., shared kernel versions, common upstream APIs, or identical orchestration layers). A monolith with high total redundancy but homogeneous configuration collapses under a single systemic bug, whereas a decentralized network with heterogeneous substrates acts as a set of natural firewalls.","optional_refs":["a426de915dea51b12979c2b1d434573e9e278f8f69d276a343a03b3b8370086f","b4f9f4905f5f5415b86521de0b35e7f642768bc4a21f38161ca5e16f14e8cd43"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.9},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"True independence of failure is distinguished from apparent independence by the temporal synchronization of the failure-recovery cycle.","counterconditions":["The failure is a cascading event where an independent trigger in one service causes a deterministic, rapid-fire collapse of others via a dependency chain.","The recovery is gated by a shared external resource that creates an artificial synchronization point during the reboot phase."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"The 'Recovery Synchronization' discriminator: if independent services fail and recover within a tight, synchronized temporal window (e.g., 10ms), the probability of independent failure is statistically zero, indicating a latent shared trigger (like a leap second or a global DNS outage). True independence manifests as stochastic, staggered failure and recovery times across the network.","optional_refs":["c96c1b10dceb3dc2640b8839667b59a0a24486972a50efd6f0a56f2565b59d4a"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.8},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"Decentralized networks are fundamentally more fragile to correlated failures because economic pressures force small operators toward a 'free-tier' dependency convergence.","counterconditions":["The decentralized network consists of high-resource operators with idiosyncratic, bespoke infrastructure.","The monolith's internal redundancy is strictly homogeneous (identical binaries/configs), making it more vulnerable than even a converged decentralized network."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Economic Convergence Trap: Small operators minimize costs by using the same few popular, free-tier managed services. This creates a high-density intersection in the dependency graph. While the services are 'independently operated,' they share a critical failure point that a large, well-funded monolith would have mitigated through custom-built, proprietary, or diversified internal infrastructure.","optional_refs":["415d2e3611b5286555565611681c7509770019a2c9313733bb2e6fe230320065","92ac737a15d26217e53d6256c636d6a0a4fb0022fc95c553d4951ada969250d6"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.6},{"checker_specs":[],"claim":"The 'strictly more total internal redundancy' of a monolith is a vanity metric that actually increases the probability of correlated failure by expanding the systemic attack surface.","counterconditions":["The monolith implements diverse redundancy (e.g., different OSes, different languages for the same logic).","The decentralized network's lack of redundancy makes it so fragile that individual failures trigger a systemic collapse via cascading load."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Homogeneity Amplification: In a monolith, redundancy usually implies replication of the same state and logic. This ensures that any bug triggering a failure in one node is present in all redundant nodes. A decentralized network's 'inefficiency' (fragmented, sub-optimal setups) provides the heterogeneity necessary to prevent a single exploit or bug from achieving 100% penetration.","optional_refs":["d31ae7efe31c4a412309646f27b0037fb815aea7c97f4f6a82971dc3b37bb08c"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":[],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.4}],"scratch_proposal":{"cluster_suggestions":[],"links":[],"new_blocks":[{"body":{"content":"Hypothesizing a 'Resilience Paradox': The effort to make decentralized systems 'professional' (via standardizing on Terraform, Helm, etc.) is exactly what kills their resilience by mapping the dependency graph to a single point of failure. True resilience may require 'intentional operational chaos'—forcing operators to use different versions of tools to prevent the virtual monolith effect.","why_keep_this":"Explores the counter-intuitive idea that standardization is a risk factor for decentralized resilience."},"local_key":"NEW_001"}],"revisions":[],"unresolved_questions":[{"question":"Can we derive a formal 'Diversity Coefficient' that quantifies the probability of a correlated failure based on the overlap of dependency trees across a network?","related_refs":["SCR_007","SCR_014"]}]},"simulation_proposals":[]}